  "default_direction": "en_to_pl",
  "note": "A2 liść · rides a1_inst_job (inst_identity). Written for THIS learner: he is English, so Jestem Anglikiem / Nie mówię po polsku / Mieszkam w Anglii are the spine, not a neutral country list. Three deliberate calls, all reported to James: (1) CAPITALISATION is taught as content — Polska/Polak/Anglik take a capital, polski/angielski do not. The pair Polska (Poland) vs polska książka (a Polish book) carries it, with an explain on each. (2) FEMININE NATIONALITIES Polka and Angielka ARE included — the -ka/-ką slot is already fully known from a1_inst_job (nauczycielka/nauczycielką), so they are new nouns in an old frame, not a new system; feminines for the German/French/Czech nationalities are NOT included (those nationality nouns are not taught here at all, only the countries). (3) po polsku / po angielsku ARE taught, as OPAQUE WHOLE CHUNKS — never analysed, never presented as related to the adjective. They are declared as multi-word lemmas (the house mechanism, cf. 'w domu', 'pada deszcz'), so no new structure ID was invented. Reason: Nie mówię po polsku is the single most useful sentence an English beginner owns in Poland; leaving it out to protect a rule he will never be asked to state would be the wrong trade. Niemcy and Czechy are plural-only → gender pl → są, not jest; that is drilled in two sentences and one explain. NO extra countries were added beyond the brief's ten: the pack already carries a capitalisation rule, an instrumental nationality set and two opaque chunks, and load-splitting outranks breadth. stolica was left unused — every clean frame for it needed a second unknown (Warszawa, or the genitive Polski which collides visually with the adjective polski). Adjectives take no gender badge (that field is for nouns).",
